The young Goddess walks on the surface of her sparkling waters, [pause=0.1]pondering her past yet finding nothing in the depths of her memories.[pause=0.8] She walks towards the small fishing town from which she was born, watching an old fisherman preparing his vessel.[pause=0.] The man’s eyes seem to be full of history and experience.
Seyche: Good morning, dear fisherman.
Fisherman: Ah, [pause=0.5]my Goddess... [pause=0.5]I did not expect you to suddenly appear in front of a fisherman like me.
The fisherman stops for a moment upon noticing Seyche’s presence and places his crates on the dock.
Seyche: Well,[pause=0.2] there is a reason as to why,[pause=0.2] and it’s because I need to ask you something.
Seyche: You seem like someone who has lived a long life,[pause=0.5] full of stories and adventures I’m sure.[pause=0.8] I was wondering whether you could tell me a little about the old sea Goddess.
Fisherman: Ah, [lspeed=0.1]well... [lspeed]I don’t know any specific history behind the old civilization that lived here, [pause=0.2]but all I know is the tale my forefathers have told me of this place.
Seyche: Oh?[pause=0.8] Do tell.
Fisherman: They say that centuries ago,[pause=0.1] there was a great storm that raged on for several moons and buried the old cities by the coast.
Seyche: I see…[pause=0.6] is there anything else that you know?
Fisherman: I’m afraid not, my goddess.
Seyche: Very well.[pause=0.5] I thank you regardless.